ss=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"bbhFh8HMsD"},{"type":"text","value":"—which is ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"PyPcQldpXD"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"not","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"G4TxMDLbu7"}],"key":"BNyldmNrY5"},{"type":"text","value":" the same as accepting ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"ZcyvUozGaS"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"H_0","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"CO6qp8L3H8"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"ev9u9KRWQh"}],"key":"qw6fV22LO8"}],"key":"gwMYuL5yo7"}],"key":"VsqzGNqy6G"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Video","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"kP9o1GYcTj"}],"key":"AZ5ksh4efA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GzkWcsJyPH4","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hypothesis Testing","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"dXBqWGdd1g"}],"urlSource":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GzkWcsJyPH4","key":"eAAzIVQbdE"}],"key":"PGvZdyRHms"}],"key":"y4nc51lS1G"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: The Hypothesis-Testing Process","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"iBtahTSIq1"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-the-hypothesis-testing-process","label":"Check Your Understanding: The Hypothesis-Testing Process","html_id":"check-your-understanding-the-hypothesis-testing-process","implicit":true,"key":"UOWt2HCVxr"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Background:","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"h4Be7KbPdF"}],"key":"Uega23NYQ1"},{"type":"text","value":" Based on the National Center for Health Statistics, the proportion of babies born at low birth weight (below 2,500 grams) in the United States is roughly 0.078, or 7.8% (based on all the births in the United States in the year 2002). A study was done in order to check whether smoking by pregnant women increases the risk of low birth weight. In other words, the researchers wanted to check whether the proportion of babies born at low birth weight among women who smoked during their pregnancy is higher than the proportion in the general population. The researchers followed a sample of 400 women who had smoked during their pregnancy and recorded the birth weight of the newborns. Based on the data, the p-value was found to be 0.016.","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"AZ9yQFI7dQ"}],"key":"jmU4tITPpN"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-30a9c49f85f8d76895c10687ac28287f.mjs","model":{"question":"What are the hypotheses being tested in this study?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"$H_0$: the proportion among smokers is 0.078; $H_a$: the proportion among smokers is higher than 0.078","feedback":"Correct! $H_0$: the proportion of low-birth-weight babies among smokers is 0.078 (same as the general population); $H_a$: the proportion is higher than 0.078."},{"correct":false,"text":"$H_0$: the proportion among smokers is higher than 0.078; $H_a$: the proportion is 0.078","feedback":"This reverses the null and alternative hypotheses."},{"correct":false,"text":"$H_0$: the proportion among smokers is 0.078; $H_a$: the proportion is not 0.078","feedback":"The researchers specifically suspected an INCREASED risk, so the alternative is one-sided (higher), not two-sided."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The null hypothesis says smokers are no different from the general population.","explanation":""},"key":"fzJZJiMJ3Y"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-30a9c49f85f8d76895c10687ac28287f.mjs","model":{"question":"Using a 0.05 significance level, what is the correct conclusion from the p-value of 0.016?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Reject $H_0$ and conclude that the proportion of low-birth-weight babies among smokers is higher than 7.8%","feedback":"Correct! Since $0.016 < 0.05$, we reject $H_0$ and conclude that smoking during pregnancy is associated with an increased proportion of low-birth-weight babies."},{"correct":false,"text":"Do not reject $H_0$—the evidence is not strong enough","feedback":"0.016 is smaller than 0.05, so the data DO provide significant evidence against $H_0$."},{"correct":false,"text":"Reject $H_0$ (no further conclusion is needed)","feedback":"The conclusion must be drawn in context, not just stated symbolically."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Compare 0.016 with 0.05, and state the conclusion in context.","explanation":""},"key":"w3qICLgtHG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same researchers also wanted to examine whether second-hand smoking (exposure to another person smoking) by pregnant women increases the risk of low birth weight (i.e., whether the proportion of babies born at a low birth weight among women who were second-hand smokers during their pregnancy is higher than the proportion in the general population). The researchers obtained a sample of 175 pregnant women who were second-hand smokers, followed them during their pregnancies, and found that 10.2% of the newborns had low birth weight. Based on these data, the p-value was found to be 0.119.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"JcJlQwrIiS"}],"key":"yAOcGdGpLL"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-30a9c49f85f8d76895c10687ac28287f.mjs","model":{"question":"What is the correct interpretation of the p-value of 0.119?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"If the true proportion were 7.8%, there would be a 0.119 chance of a sample proportion of 10.2% or higher","feedback":"Correct! If the true proportion among second-hand smokers were 0.078, there would be a 0.119 probability of getting a sample proportion of 10.2% or higher in a sample of 175."},{"correct":false,"text":"There is a 0.119 probability that second-hand smoking has no effect","feedback":"The p-value is not the probability that $H_0$ is true."},{"correct":false,"text":"11.9% of the babies in the sample had low birth weight","feedback":"The p-value is not the proportion of low-birth-weight babies—that was 10.2%."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The p-value assumes $H_0$ (proportion = 0.078) is true.","explanation":""},"key":"iIgmpKLdCS"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-30a9c49f85f8d76895c10687ac28287f.mjs","model":{"question":"Using a 0.05 significance level, what is the correct conclusion for the second-hand smoking study?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Do not reject $H_0$—the data do not provide enough evidence that second-hand smoking increases the risk","feedback":"Correct! Since $0.119 > 0.05$, the data do not provide enough evidence to conclude that second-hand smoking increases the risk of low birth weight."},{"correct":false,"text":"Reject $H_0$ and conclude that second-hand smoking increases the risk","feedback":"The p-value exceeds 0.05, so the results are NOT statistically significant."},{"correct":false,"text":"Accept $H_0$ and conclude that second-hand smoking has no effect on birth weight","feedback":"Failing to reject $H_0$ does not prove that second-hand smoking has no effect—we simply lack sufficient evidence of one."}],"multi":false,"hint":"0.119 > 0.05—and remember what failing to reject does and does not mean.","explanation":""},"key":"QnOpOikGsJ"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-30a9c49f85f8d76895c10687ac28287f.mjs","model":{"question":"Even though the sample proportion among second-hand smokers (10.2%) was above 7.8%, the study did not reach a significant result, while the smoking study did. Which factor best explains why a sample result can fail to be significant?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"The observed difference was small enough, and the sample small enough, that chance alone could plausibly explain it","feedback":"Correct! With a smaller sample (175 vs. 400) and a modest difference, a sample proportion of 10.2% is not surprising enough under $H_0$—chance alone could reasonably produce it."},{"correct":false,"text":"The sample proportion went in the wrong direction","feedback":"The direction of the difference was consistent with $H_a$; the issue is the strength of the evidence, not its direction."},{"correct":false,"text":"The p-value must have been miscalculated","feedback":"The p-value was computed correctly—non-significance reflects insufficient evidence, not an error."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Think about what makes an observed difference \"surprising\" under $H_0$—both the size of the difference and the sample size matter.","explanation":""},"key":"YMN2TDwSnk"}],"key":"BQHVpcN6lN"}],"key":"janaf4xAKW"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Significance Level and Drawing Conclusions","url":"/pages/p200-m15-overview-4-of-4","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"},"next":{"title":"The z-Test for a Proportion: Overview","url":"/pages/p202-m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-pro","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}